The `_unwrapped_call` implementation is:

```python
    @cached_property
    def _unwrapped_call(self) -> Any:\n        if self.call is None:\n            return self.call  # pragma: no cover\n        unwrapped = inspect.unwrap(self.call)\n        if isinstance(unwrapped, partial):\n            unwrapped = unwrapped.func\n        return unwrapped
```

This only unwraps once and then checks for `partial`. But what if it's `partial(wrapped_func)`? `inspect.unwrap` might not unwrap `partial`.

Actually, `inspect.unwrap` follows `__wrapped__` chain. `functools.partial` does NOT set `__wrapped__`.

So if we have `partial(wrap(func))`, `inspect.unwrap` will see `partial` and stop. Then we check `isinstance(unwrapped, partial)` and get `wrap(func)`. But `wrap(func)` might still be wrapped!

And if we have `wrap(partial(func))`, `inspect.unwrap` will get `partial(func)`. Then we check `isinstance(unwrapped, partial)` and get `func`. This case works.

But if we have `partial(wrap(partial(func)))`, it fails.

The problem statement says: "The logic to analyze if a function is an async, generator, async generator, callable instance, etc. now also checks and unwraps and extracts partials in all those levels."

So we need a loop to keep unwrapping and extracting partials until we can't anymore.

